{"version":1,"claims":[{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Goldstein2015ARFM","verdict":"unverified","source":"none","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:06:23Z","response_hash":"","fields":{"author":"Goldstein, Raymond E","journal":"Annu. Rev. Fluid Mech.","pages":"343-375","title":"Green Algae as Model Organisms for Biological Fluid Mechanics","volume":"47","year":"2015"},"diffs":[],"failure_note":"not found at the registries — books published before DOIs often are not registered; the entry may still be correct"},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Goldstein2016JFM","verdict":"unverified","source":"none","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:06:23Z","response_hash":"","fields":{"author":"Goldstein, Raymond E","journal":"J. Fluid Mech.","pages":"1-39","title":"Batchelor Prize Lecture. Fluid Dynamics at the Scale of the Cell","volume":"807","year":"2016"},"diffs":[],"failure_note":"not found at the registries — books published before DOIs often are not registered; the entry may still be correct"},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Grossberg2007","verdict":"verified","source":"crossref-query","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:06:23Z","response_hash":"d0ce07d44d62a0b8","fields":{"author":"Grosberg, Richard K., and Strathmann, Richard R.","date-added":"2019-08-10 18:34:05 +0100","date-modified":"2019-08-10 18:35:20 +0100","journal":"Annu. Rev. Ecol. Evol. Syst.","pages":"621-654","title":"The evolution of multicellularity: a minor major transition?","volume":"38","year":"2007","bdsk-file-1":""},"diffs":[]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Huxley1912","verdict":"verified","source":"crossref-query","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:06:23Z","response_hash":"d75d1b9dbe650cac","fields":{"author":"Huxley, J.S.","publisher":"Cambridge, UK, Cambridge University Press","title":"The Individual in the Animal Kingdom","year":"1912"},"diffs":[]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Itoh2019","verdict":"verified","source":"crossref-query","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:06:23Z","response_hash":"d3820e7ec3f5f965","fields":{"author":"Ito, Hiroaki and Omori, Toshihiro and Ishikawa, Takuji","date-added":"2019-08-10 18:59:10 +0100","date-modified":"2019-09-12 16:37:47 +0100","journal":"J. Fluid Mech.","pages":"774-796","title":"Swimming mediated by ciliary beating: comparison with a squirmer model","volume":"874","year":"2019"},"diffs":[]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Josef2005","verdict":"mismatch","source":"openalex","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:06:23Z","response_hash":"c6e86288934b275c","fields":{"abstract":"With an instrument that can record the motion of both cilia of the unicellular alga Chlamydomonas reinhardtii for many hours, the behavioral differences of its two cilia have been studied to determine their specific role in phototaxis. The organism was held on a fixed micropipette with the plane of ciliary beating rotated into the imaging plane of a quadrant photodetector. The responses to square-wave light patterns of a wide range of temporal frequencies were used to characterize the responses of each cilium. Eighty-one cells were examined showing an unexpectedly diverse range of responses. Plausible common signals for the linear and nonlinear signals from the cell body are suggested. Three independent ciliary measures--the beat frequency, stroke velocity, and phasing of the two cilia--have been identified. The cell body communicates to the cilia the direction of phototaxis the cell desires to go, the absolute light intensity, and the appropriate graded transient response for tracking the light source. The complexity revealed by each measure of the ciliary response indicates many independent variables are involved in the net phototactic response. In spite of their morphological similarity, the two cilia of Chlamydomonas respond uniquely. Probably the signals from the cell body fan out to independent pathways in the cilia. Each cilium modifies the input in its own way. The change in the pattern of the effective and recovery strokes of each cilium associated with negative phototaxis has been demonstrated and its involvement in phototactic turning is described.","author":"Josef, Keith and Saranak, Jureepan and Foster, Kenneth W","doi":"10.1002/cm.20069","file":":C$\\backslash$:/Users/User/Documents/Mendeley Desktop/Josef, Saranak, Foster{\\_}2005.pdf:pdf","journal":"Cell Mot. Cytoskeleton","keywords":"Animals,Automatic Data Processing,Chlamydomonas reinhardtii,Chlamydomonas reinhardtii: cytology,Chlamydomonas reinhardtii: physiology,Chlamydomonas reinhardtii: radiation effects,Cilia,Cilia: physiology,Cilia: ultrastructure,Light,Light Signal Transduction,Light Signal Transduction: physiology,Light Signal Transduction: radiation effects,Locomotion,Locomotion: physiology,Locomotion: radiation effects,Nonlinear Dynamics,Photic Stimulation,Phototropism,Phototropism: physiology,Phototropism: radiation effects,Signal Transduction,Signal Transduction: physiology,Signal Transduction: radiation effects","month":"jun","number":"2","pages":"97--111","title":"{Ciliary behavior of a negatively phototactic \\textit{Chlamydomonas reinhardtii.}}","volume":"61","year":"2005"},"diffs":[{"field":"title","in_document":"{Ciliary behavior of a negatively phototactic \\textit{Chlamydomonas reinhardtii.}}","in_source":"Ciliary behavior of a negatively phototactic Chlamydomonas reinhardtii","severity":"medium"}]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Josef2006","verdict":"mismatch","source":"openalex","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:06:23Z","response_hash":"c6e86288934b275c","fields":{"abstract":"In response to light stimulation Chlamydomonas reinhardtii changes the beating frequency, beating pattern, and beating synchrony of the trans and cis cilia to steer the freely-swimming cell relative to light sources. To understand the cell steering behavior the impulse responses of the beating frequency and stroke velocity of each cilium have been obtained with high temporal resolution on cells held with a micropipette. Interestingly the response of each cilium is quite different. The trans cilium responds with less delay than the cis cilium for both beating frequency and stroke velocity. For light stimulation at 2 Hz, the critical cell-rotation frequency, both responses of the trans and cis cilia are about 180 degrees out of phase. The trans-cilium beating frequency response peaks at a stimulus frequency of 5-6 Hz, higher than the cis at 1-2 Hz. The stroke velocities of the trans and cis cilia have the same stimulus-frequency response (2 Hz), but the trans cilium has a shorter delay than the cis. The times to maximum response are much shorter than the time for a rotation of the cell. The use of two different approaches that enable the trans cilium to respond ahead of the cis for both the beating frequency and stroke velocity responses suggests the importance of both responses to phototaxis. Internal cell processing responsible for the time course of the responses is proposed.","author":"Josef, Keith and Saranak, Jureepan and Foster, Kenneth W","doi":"10.1002/cm.20158","file":":C$\\backslash$:/Users/User/Documents/Mendeley Desktop/Josef, Saranak, Foster{\\_}2006.pdf:pdf","issn":"0886-1544","journal":"Cell Mot. Cytoskeleton","keywords":"Animals,Chlamydomonas reinhardtii,Chlamydomonas reinhardtii: cytology,Chlamydomonas reinhardtii: physiology,Chlamydomonas reinhardtii: radiation effects,Cilia,Cilia: physiology,Cilia: ultrastructure,Light,Light Signal Transduction,Light Signal Transduction: physiology,Light Signal Transduction: radiation effects,Locomotion,Locomotion: physiology,Locomotion: radiation effects,Nonlinear Dynamics,Photic Stimulation,Signal Transduction,Signal Transduction: physiology,Signal Transduction: radiation effects","mendeley-groups":"Chlamydomonas/Motility and Behavior/Phototaxis","number":"12","pages":"758--777","pmid":"16986140","title":"{Linear systems analysis of the ciliary steering behavior associated with negative-phototaxis in \\textit{Chlamydomonas reinhardtii.}}","volume":"63","year":"2006"},"diffs":[{"field":"title","in_document":"{Linear systems analysis of the ciliary steering behavior associated with negative-phototaxis in \\textit{Chlamydomonas reinhardtii.}}","in_source":"Linear systems analysis of the ciliary steering behavior associated with negative‐phototaxis in Chlamydomonas reinhardtii","severity":"medium"}]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Kirkbook1998","verdict":"partial","source":"crossref-query","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:06:23Z","response_hash":"400c3e0dbaab8892","fields":{"author":"Kirk, D.L.","publisher":"Cambridge, UK, Cambridge University Press","title":"Volvox. Molecular-Genetic Origins of Multicellularity and Cellular Differentiation","year":"1998"},"diffs":[{"field":"authors","in_document":"Kirk, D.L.","in_source":"Annette W. Coleman","severity":"high"}]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Lighthill1952","verdict":"verified","source":"crossref-query","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:06:23Z","response_hash":"3beb62dee0ced48c","fields":{"author":"Lighthill, M.~J.","date-modified":"2015-04-01 15:36:06 +0000","journal":"Comm. Pure Appl. Math.","month":"may","pages":"109--118","title":"{On the squirming motion of nearly spherical deformable bodies through liquids at very small Reynolds numbers}","volume":"5","year":"1952"},"diffs":[]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Schaller1997","verdict":"mismatch","source":"openalex","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:06:23Z","response_hash":"c6e86288934b275c","fields":{"abstract":"By using a real-time assay that allows measurement of the phototactic orientation of the unicellular alga Chlamydomonas with millisecond time resolution, it can be shown that single photons not only induce transient direction changes but that fluence rates as low as 1 photon cell(-1) s(-1) can already lead to a persistent orientation. Orientation is a binary variable, i.e., in a partially oriented population some organisms are fully oriented while the rest are still at random. Action spectra reveal that the response to a pulsed stimulus follows the Dartnall-nomogram for a rhodopsin while the response to a persistent stimulus falls off more rapidly toward the red end of the spectrum. Thus light of 540 nm, for which chlamy-rhodopsin is equally sensitive as for 440-nm light, induces no measurable persistent orientation while 440-nm light does. A model is presented which explains not only this behavior, but also how Chlamydomonas can track the light direction and switches between a positive and negative phototaxis. According to the model the ability to detect the direction of light, to make the right turn and to stay oriented, is a direct consequence of the helical path of the organism, the orientation of its eyespot relative to the helix-axis, and the special shielding properties of eyespot and cell body. The model places particular emphasis on the fact that prolonged swimming into the correct direction not only requires making a correct turn initially, but also avoiding further turns once the right direction has been reached.","author":"Schaller, K and David, R and Uhl, R","doi":"10.1016/S0006-3495(97)78188-8","file":":C$\\backslash$:/Users/User/Documents/Mendeley Desktop/Schaller, David, Uhl{\\_}1997.pdf:pdf","issn":"0006-3495","journal":"Biophys. J.","keywords":"Animals,Cell Movement,Chlamydomonas reinhardtii,Chlamydomonas reinhardtii: physiology,Chlamydomonas reinhardtii: radiation effects,Darkness,Flagella,Flagella: physiology,Light,Microscopy,Orientation,Radiation,Reproducibility of Results,Scattering,Software,Spectrophotometry,Spectrophotometry: instrumentation,Spectrophotometry: methods,Video,Video: instrumentation,Video: methods","mendeley-groups":"Chlamydomonas/Motility and Behavior/Phototaxis","number":"3","pages":"1562--1572","pmid":"9284323","title":"{How \\textit{Chlamydomonas} keeps track of the light once it has reached the right phototactic orientation.}","volume":"73","year":"1997"},"diffs":[{"field":"title","in_document":"{How \\textit{Chlamydomonas} keeps track of the light once it has reached the right phototactic orientation.}","in_source":"How Chlamydomonas keeps track of the light once it has reached the right phototactic orientation","severity":"medium"}]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Weismann1892","verdict":"unverified","source":"none","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:06:23Z","response_hash":"","fields":{"author":"Weismann, A.","publisher":"Oxford, UK, Clarendon Press","title":"Essays on Heredity and Kindred Biological Problems","year":"1892"},"diffs":[],"failure_note":"not found at the registries — books published before DOIs often are not registered; the entry may still be correct"},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"Yoshimura2001","verdict":"mismatch","source":"openalex","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:06:23Z","response_hash":"c6e86288934b275c","fields":{"author":"Yoshimura, K. and Kamiya, Ritsu","doi":"10.1093/pcp/pce084","file":":C$\\backslash$:/Users/User/Documents/Mendeley Desktop/Yoshimura, Kamiya{\\_}2001.pdf:pdf","issn":"14719053","journal":"Plant Cell Phys.","mendeley-groups":"Chlamydomonas/Motility and Behavior/Phototaxis","month":"jun","number":"6","pages":"665--672","publisher":"Oxford University Press","title":"{The sensitivity of \\textit{Chlamydomonas} photoreceptor is optimized for the frequency of cell body rotation}","volume":"42","year":"2001"},"diffs":[{"field":"title","in_document":"{The sensitivity of \\textit{Chlamydomonas} photoreceptor is optimized for the frequency of cell body rotation}","in_source":"The Sensitivity of Chlamydomonas Photoreceptor is Optimized for the Frequency of Cell Body Rotation","severity":"medium"}]},{"kind":"bib-entry","target":"arakaki2013","verdict":"verified","source":"crossref-query","fetched_at":"2026-09-12T00:06:23Z","response_hash":"dae46c885431bcc1","fields":{"author":"Arakaki, Yoko and Kawai-Toyooka, Hiroko and Hamamura, Yuki and Higashiyama, Tetsuya and Noga, Akira and Hirono, Masafumi and Olson, Bradley J.S.C. and Nozaki, Hisayoshi","date-added":"2019-08-10 18:12:22 +0100","date-modified":"2019-08-10 18:16:59 +0100","journal":"PLoS One","nu